# Build context hygiene.
#
# The build context is the repository root, not web/, because pnpm-workspace.yaml
# and pnpm-lock.yaml live here: a context of web/ would either fail
# --frozen-lockfile or install a dependency tree that differs from CI's.
#
# .env is excluded because the entire context is sent to the daemon, and anything
# in the context can end up in the build cache. This is necessary and NOT
# sufficient: `next build` writes its own copy of web/.env into
# .next/standalone/web/.env, which no context filter can reach, so the Dockerfile
# also deletes it after copying. Both halves are required; a context filter alone
# leaves credentials in the published image. See the plan's section 14.4.

.git
.gitignore
.github

node_modules
**/node_modules

# Build output. Rebuilt inside the image; copying it in would let a stale build
# be published as if it were fresh, and would also defeat layer caching.
**/.next
**/dist

# Contract build output. The app consumes only the exported ABI, which is
# checked in under web/src/lib/contracts/ -- so the image never needs Hardhat's
# artifacts, and keeping them out also keeps the context small.
**/artifacts
**/cache
**/typechain-types

**/coverage
**/*.log
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Secrets. .env.example stays so the image and the repository agree on which
# variables exist; it holds names and placeholders, never values.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Documentation and editor state are not read by the build.
docs
**/*.md
**/.vscode
.gitmsg*
